# Data access

Repositories use `JdbcTemplate` directly. No JPA entities, no Spring Data repositories,
no query DSL.

- One `private static final RowMapper<T>` per repository, mapping to the domain `record`.
- Always bind parameters with `?` placeholders. The only string that may be spliced into
  SQL is a generated placeholder list (`String.join(",", Collections.nCopies(n, "?"))`)
  — never a value, column, or user-supplied fragment.
- Build conditional queries with a `StringBuilder` seeded `WHERE 1=1` plus a parallel
  argument list, as in @src/main/java/com/kindlerss/repository/ArticleRepository.java.
- SQL lives in text blocks, keywords uppercase.
- Timestamps cross the boundary as `Instant`; convert with `Timestamp.from` on the way in
  and `Timestamp::toInstant` on the way out, guarding for `null`.
- Inserts that can race use `ON CONFLICT ... DO NOTHING` and report the outcome (a
  generated key, or the number of affected rows) rather than throwing.
- Bulk updates return how many rows actually changed, so callers can report a count —
  add `AND col <> ?` so unchanged rows are not counted.
- `updated_at = NOW()` on every `UPDATE`.

## Migrations

Flyway runs on startup. Migrations are forward-only and immutable: never edit a
migration that has been applied anywhere, add `V<n>__<description>.sql` instead. Keep
them runnable against PostgreSQL 16+ and idempotent where cheap. Add the matching
coverage to `PostgresRepositoryTest`, which starts an embedded PostgreSQL.
